1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,204
D S R F Q — automated quoting for machined parts.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:13,883
A customer sends a drawing and a 3D model. Traditionally an
estimator reads it and prices it by hand — hours per part.

3
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:22,523
Here the work is already done: conversion, title block reading,
costing and ballooning.

4
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:28,229
The geometry is measured straight from the STEP file.

5
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:34,949
Then priced: setup, roughing, finishing. Twelve fifty.

6
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:40,334
It reads the parts list off the drawing — nine items, with part
numbers.

7
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:48,478
And the special processes: cleaning and silver plating, each
with its specification.

8
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:58,183
This is the customer's original sheet. Their title block is
covered here, because replacing it is what the next step does.

9
00:00:59,690 --> 00:01:08,413
The converted drawing. Same geometry, our title block, notes
rewritten to our specifications.

10
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:21,978
It also balloons the drawing for inspection. A hundred and
seventy-two balloons across five pages, each tied to a dimension
and its tolerance — the mark-up a quality team would otherwise

11
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:34,513
Every file stays searchable by what is inside it — part number,
material, customer, all read from the drawing itself.

12
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:47,804
And across the system: how long each stage takes, and what
failed. Two files in, a costed quote out.

